In C++, a reference is an alias for an existing object and is commonly used to avoid copying when passing arguments. A const reference can read an object without modifying it.
```cpp
void show(const std::string& s) {
    std::cout << s << '\n';
}
int main() {
    std::string name = "Ada";
    show(name);
}
```
The `&` in the parameter declares a reference. `const` prevents the function from changing the referred-to string through that parameter.
